Split-KV attention for speculative-decode batches on the FLASH_ATTN backend
(vLLM 0.27.1, Ampere), with query-row tiling so the verify block can be longer
than the drafter's.

FlashAttention-2 only splits the KV sequence across thread blocks when a request
has exactly one query token; with MTP (k+1 = 5 queries) a 24-head model gets 24
blocks on the 3090's 82 SMs and each attention layer takes ~57 us for a 1.5k
context. The Triton kernel in v1/attention/ops/spec_decode_attn.py gives every
(request, kv-head, query tile) NUM_SEGMENTS blocks with an online-softmax partial
and a combine step. Enabled with VLLM_SPEC_DECODE_ATTN=1 (bf16 KV cache only, no
sliding window / softcap / alibi).

The query rows of one request (q_len x G of them, G = query heads per kv head)
are now split into tiles of BLOCK_M rows instead of having to fit in one, so the
kernel is not capped at BLOCK_M // G query tokens. That cap is what made a verify
block longer than the drafter's unaffordable: a 16-token block fell back to FA2
and doubled the step at 25k context. Measured per attention layer, batch 1,
25k-token context: 250 / 583 / 1,132 us at 8 / 16 / 32 query tokens, against
FA2's 2,085 / 2,053 / 1,997 us. BLOCK_M is chosen to keep the tile count at 1
where the register budget allows (128 rows with 8 warps), since every extra tile
re-reads that request's KV segment.

The partial buffers are allocated once, for VLLM_SPEC_DECODE_ATTN_QMAX query
tokens (single-user/start_qwen.sh sets it from DFLASH_TOKENS). They must not grow
afterwards: a captured CUDA graph holds their addresses, and a later reallocation
- which is what happened as soon as a small prefill chunk asked for a longer
block - leaves the captured decode graph reading freed memory.

Apply from the repo root:
  patch -p1 -d venv/lib/python3.12/site-packages/vllm < patches/spec-decode-attn.patch

